#* {{quote-text|en|year=1644|author={{w|John Milton}}|title=Areopagitica|passage=it was first establisht and put in practice by '''Antichristian''' malice and mystery on set purpose to extinguish, if it were possible, the light of Reformation, and to settle falshood; little differing from that policie wherewith the Turk upholds his ''Alcoran'', by the prohibition of Printing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|author=Justin A. Smith|title=Commentary on the Revelation|page=240|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=f3s9AAAAYAAJ&pg=RA2-PA240|text=They gave their power and authority to the beast—lent themselves to the support of the '''antichristian''' usurpation; prompted to this partly by superstitious reverence for the spiritual authority it claimed, and partly, perhaps chiefly, by political aims and exigencies.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author=Christopher Hill|title=Antichrist in Seventeenth-Century England|page=92|text=To oppose the gathering of churches was described as siding with Antichrist in a pamphlet of 1644. Separation from an '''antichristian''' church was permissible.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Anthony Milton|title=Catholic and Reformed: The Roman and Protestant Churches in English Protestant Thought, 1600–1640|page=121|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=stwluHDJsQgC&pg=PA121|text=Thus Articles 78 and 80, which unequivocally asserted the pope's '''antichristian''' nature, were among the Irish Articles revoked in 1634.}}
